The sets of input forms grouped under Analysis Control allows you 
to adjust some parameters of the finite element model, to select 
the required level of output and to start the finite element 
analysis.
 
Use the form Mesh refinement to change the refinement level of the
finite element mesh. The more refined the mesh is, the more 
accurate the results, but also the longer the calculation time 
and the larger the memory requirement. The mesh proposed by 
default is optimal in most of the cases.
 
The user may however wish to increase the refinement level in 
order to capture highly irregular stress/strain fields caused for
instance by damage localisation. The user should be aware that the
calculation time will increase exponentially.
 
The user may wish on the contrary to decrease the mesh refinement
to reduce calculation time and memory requirements.
 
Use the form Stepping Scheme to tune the parameters of the 
solution procedure. The proposed defaults are optimal. Tuning of 
the stepping scheme should be left to specialists in non-linear 
finite element analysis.
 
Some particularly unstable analyses result in calculation failure 
due to divergence of the numerical solution scheme. this problem 
can usually be solved by adjusting the stepping scheme. increasing 
the number of time steps and/or the maximum number of iterations is 
most of the time sufficient to solve divergence problems. The user
should however be aware that the calculation time will increase 
proportionally.
  
Use the form Output Selection to select the output level. Three 
output levels are proposed. The first level selects only essential 
output, i.e. overview tables and bar charts. The second level 
selects also detailed output for the cement. The third level 
selects also detailed output for the formation and the casing.
Use the form Analysis to chose the analysis options and to start 
and monitor the finite element calculation.
 